    body: `
<p>In a Czech shop the assistant greets you with <b>Dobrý den</b>, and you answer the same way. You and the assistant are strangers, so both of you use vy.</p>
<h3>What the assistant says</h3>
<p>You may hear <b>Co chcete?</b> (What would you like?). <b>chcete</b> is the vy-form. A friend would hear <b>Co chceš?</b>, the ty-form, and you never say that to the assistant. If you only want to browse, say <b>Jen se dívám</b>; <b>se</b> sits in second place, as in <b>Jak se máte</b>. If you want help, just ask.</p>
{{ex:Co chcete?|tsoh KHTSEH-teh|What would you like?}}
{{ex:Jen se dívám.|yehn seh DYEE-vaahm|I'm just looking.}}
<h3>Asking for something</h3>
<p>Use <b>Máte…?</b> (Do you have…?) or <b>Hledám…</b> (I'm looking for…). After <b>hledám</b>, a feminine word in -a takes the object form: bunda becomes <b>bundu</b>. Neuter <b>tričko</b> stays the same. Both questions are polite on their own; add <b>prosím</b> at the end to sound warmer.</p>
{{ex:Hledám bundu.|HLEH-daahm BOON-doo|I'm looking for a jacket.}}
{{ex:Máte velké tričko?|MAAH-teh VEHL-kehh TRIHCH-koh|Do you have a large T-shirt?}}
<h3>Colors and sizes agree</h3>
<p>Colors and sizes are adjectives, so they match the gender of the noun, the same three endings as dobrý, dobrá, dobré. The color comes before the noun, as in English: <b>bílé tričko</b>. For the medium size, <b>střední</b> has one form for all three.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>masculine</th><th>feminine</th><th>neuter</th></tr><tr><td class="k">černý</td><td class="k">černá</td><td class="k">černé</td></tr><tr><td class="k">bílý</td><td class="k">bílá</td><td class="k">bílé</td></tr><tr><td class="k">malý</td><td class="k">malá</td><td class="k">malé</td></tr><tr><td class="k">velký</td><td class="k">velká</td><td class="k">velké</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:Ta bunda je černá.|tah BOON-dah yeh CHEHR-naah|The jacket is black.}}
{{ex:To tričko je bílé.|toh TRIHCH-koh yeh BEE-lehh|The T-shirt is white.}}
<p><b>kalhoty</b> (pants) and <b>boty</b> (shoes) are plural in Czech, as in English, and their colors end in -é: <b>černé kalhoty</b>, <b>bílé boty</b>.</p>
<h3>The price</h3>
<p>Ask <b>Kolik to stojí?</b> Clothes cost more than four crowns, so the answer uses <b>korun</b>: <b>sto korun</b>, <b>devadesát korun</b>. The Kč on a price tag stands for Czech crowns; read it out as korun. When you decide, say <b>Vezmu si to</b> (I'll take it), and ask for the <b>účtenka</b> (receipt) if you need one.</p>
{{ex:Kolik to stojí?|KOH-lihk toh STOH-yee|How much is it?}}
{{ex:Stojí to devadesát korun.|STOH-yee toh DEH-vah-deh-saaht KOH-roon|It's ninety crowns.}}
{{ex:Vezmu si to.|VEHZ-moo sih toh|I'll take it.}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> the ty-form to the assistant. <b>Máš velké tričko?</b> → <b>Máte velké tričko?</b> Shop staff always get the vy-form.</div>
`,
