    blurb: "Say where you were and what you did: byl jsem, byla jsem, byl jste.",
    deck: "Everyday verbs",
    body: `
<p>English "I was" and "I worked" are the same for everyone. The Czech past has two parts: a past word that shows gender, and a small word that shows the person.</p>
<h3>The past word shows gender</h3>
<p>Take the base form of the verb, drop the -t, and add -l: <b>dělat</b> → <b>dělal</b>, <b>mluvit</b> → <b>mluvil</b>. <b>být</b> also shortens its vowel: <b>byl</b>. A man says <b>byl</b>, a woman says <b>byla</b>, and a neuter thing <b>bylo</b>. <b>jít</b> is irregular: <b>šel</b> for a man, <b>šla</b> for a woman.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Verb</th><th>a man</th><th>a woman</th></tr><tr><td class="k">být</td><td class="k">byl</td><td class="k">byla</td></tr><tr><td class="k">dělat</td><td class="k">dělal</td><td class="k">dělala</td></tr><tr><td class="k">mluvit</td><td class="k">mluvil</td><td class="k">mluvila</td></tr><tr><td class="k">pracovat</td><td class="k">pracoval</td><td class="k">pracovala</td></tr><tr><td class="k">jet</td><td class="k">jel</td><td class="k">jela</td></tr></table></div>
<h3>The person word</h3>
<p>For "I" add <b>jsem</b>, and for "you" add <b>jste</b>, the same words as in "I am" and "you are". For "he" and "she" add nothing. So the man's "I was" and "you were" differ only in <b>jsem</b> and <b>jste</b>.</p>
{{ex:Byl jsem doma.|bihl ysehm DOH-mah|I was at home. (a man)}}
{{ex:Byla jsem doma.|BIH-lah ysehm DOH-mah|I was at home. (a woman)}}
{{ex:Eva byla v Brně.|EH-vah BIH-lah VBRR-nyeh|Eva was in Brno.}}
<p>With vy to one person, the person word is <b>jste</b>, but the past word stays singular and shows that person's gender: <b>byl jste</b> to a man, <b>byla jste</b> to a woman. To a group it is <b>byli jste</b>. A friend would hear <b>jsi</b>, which you only need to recognize.</p>
{{ex:Byl jste v Praze?|bihl ysteh FPRAH-zeh|Were you in Prague? (to a man)}}
{{ex:Co jste dělala?|tsoh ysteh DYEH-lah-lah|What did you do? (to a woman)}}
<h3>jsem goes second</h3>
<p><b>jsem</b> and <b>jste</b> are clitics: little unstressed words that take the second place in the sentence. If <b>včera</b> comes first, <b>jsem</b> comes right after it, and the past word follows. The same holds after a question word: in <b>Co jste dělala?</b>, <b>jste</b> comes right after <b>co</b>. Without včera, the past word itself can go first: <b>Byla jsem doma</b>.</p>
{{ex:Včera jsem pracovala.|FCHEH-rah ysehm PRAH-tsoh-vah-lah|Yesterday I worked. (a woman)}}
<h3>Saying no</h3>
<p><b>ne-</b> joins the past word and takes the beat, just as it does in the present.</p>
{{ex:Nebyl jsem doma.|NEH-bihl ysehm DOH-mah|I wasn't at home. (a man)}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> one past word for everyone. A woman who says <b>Byl jsem doma.</b> → <b>Byla jsem doma.</b> And to a stranger the person word is always jste, never jsi.</div>
`,
