    body: `
<p>This last lesson adds no new grammar. It collects the words and sentence shapes where English quietly leads you into a mistake.</p>
<h3>False friends</h3>
<p>Some Czech words look English but mean something else. Each one is a real Czech word, so people will understand you, just not the way you meant.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Czech</th><th>Looks like</th><th>Means</th></tr><tr><td class="k">host</td><td>host</td><td>a guest: the person who comes, not the one who invites</td></tr><tr><td class="k">kolej</td><td>college</td><td>a student dorm; also a rail</td></tr><tr><td class="k">aktuální</td><td>actual</td><td>current, of this moment</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:Jsem host.|ysehm hohst|I'm a guest.}}
{{ex:To je moje kolej.|toh yeh MOH-yeh KOH-lehy|That is my dorm.}}
{{ex:To je aktuální cena.|toh yeh AHK-too-aahl-nyee TSEH-nah|That's the current price.}}
<h3>No "a", no "the", but always jsem</h3>
<p>English needs "a" and "the". Czech has no word for either, so leave them out. The verb is the opposite: it is never left out. "I am a student" is <b>Jsem student</b>, with jsem and no word for "a". <b>Já student</b> is not a sentence. <b>To je káva</b> means "This is coffee" or "This is a coffee": the situation does the work of "a" and "the". Gender still shows, though, through ten, ta and to: <b>ten čaj</b>, <b>ta káva</b>, <b>to pivo</b>.</p>
{{ex:Jsem student.|ysehm STOO-dehnt|I am a student.}}
<h3>"I like": the person is mi</h3>
<p>In English, "I" is the subject of "like". In <b>líbí se mi</b> and <b>chutná mi</b>, the thing you like is the subject, and you are <b>mi</b>. With <b>mám rád</b>, the word <b>rád</b> or <b>ráda</b> matches the person speaking, not the thing. So a woman says <b>Mám ráda čaj</b>, whatever the drink. Use <b>chutná mi</b> for food you taste and <b>líbí se mi</b> for a place, a thing or a person.</p>
{{ex:Líbí se mi Praha.|LEE-bee seh mih PRAH-hah|I like Prague.}}
{{ex:Mám ráda čaj.|maahm RAAH-dah chahy|I like tea. (a woman)}}
<h3>Crowns and strangers</h3>
<p>After five, the form is <b>korun</b>: <b>pět korun</b>, <b>sto korun</b>. Prices are where this shows up most. And to anyone you do not know, the verb ends in -te: <b>jste</b>, <b>máte</b>, <b>mluvíte</b>. The friend forms <b>jsi</b>, <b>máš</b> and <b>mluvíš</b> are ones you only need to recognize.</p>
{{ex:pět korun|pyeht KOH-roon|five crowns}}
{{ex:Mluvíte česky?|MLOO-vee-teh CHEHS-kih|Do you speak Czech?}}
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> copying the English "I like". <b>Já líbím Praha.</b> → <b>Líbí se mi Praha.</b> Prague is the subject, and you are mi.</div>
`,
